    "title": "A watch vs. a warning: Pete explains the difference",
    "text": "Mon, April 13, 2026 at 11:46 AMUpdated Thu, April 16, 2026 at 9:00 AMWatch and Warning DifferenceThe difference between a watch and a warning(WLUK) -- Monday is the first day of Tornado and Severe Weather Awareness Week in Wisconsin.Watches and warnings are often issued due to severe weather in our area. Here's the difference between the two.Watch and Warning DifferenceFOX 11 Director of Meteorology kicks off Wisconsin's Severe Weather Awareness Week by explaining the difference between a watch and a warning. (WLUK)A watch is issued by the Storm Prediction Center, usually hours before the event. It's issued because they want you to be on alert, as conditions are right for severe weather to occur.A watch is issued for a longer time period and a larger area A warning is issued by a local National Weather Service office. That's when severe weather occurring is imminent, and it's issued for a particular area for a short duration of time. A tornado or severe thunderstorm warning is issued for a smaller area and shorter time{ }A watch may encompass many counties in the state and indicates a risk of hazardous weather anywhere in that area. It means you should be vigilant and pay attention to what's happening weather-wise.If you can safely take a photo or video of severe weather where you are, share it with us here:But when a warning is issued, that's when you should go to your safe place.2026 Severe Weather Awareness WeekStorm Prediction Center updates severe outlooks with new CIG 1-3 intensity groupsBrown County highlights safety tips as part of Tornado & Severe Weather Awareness WeekWisconsin's Severe Weather Awareness Week is April 13-17What is a derecho? Here's why straight-line winds can cause more damage than tornadoesThe difference between a watch and a warningA warning means to take action now if you're in the affected area, whether it be a severe thunderstorm warning or a tornado warning.",
